Engraving with gems:

the following are "hard" gems, and can thus be used to engrave:
diamond, ruby, sapphire, topaz, emerald, aquamarine, garnet
white, red, blue, yellow brown, green, green, red
(N.B. the code in engrave.c states that the floors are granite and
thus have a hardness of ~7 => anything with a hardness >= 8 should
engrave into it.  there is a discrepancy here, as the hardness of
garnet is 7.25, yet it engraves.  i guess it's in the "variability"
of the garnet hardness, which may vary from 6.5-8.)
note that when engraving with gems/rings, it takes one turn per
letter to engrave.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Throwing gems to unicorns:

throwing a gem to a unicorn can change your luck in one of several
ways, depending on the type of gem, its identified status, and the  type
of unicorn:

 gem      unicorn align.    gem type                   luck
status     co    nonco    gem   glass   message       change
--------------------------------------------------------------
unnamed    X               X            gratefully      +1
                   X       X            hesitatingly  -1 to +1
           X                      X     graciously       0
                   X              X     graciously       0

named      X               X            gratefully      +2
                   X       X            hesitatingly  -1 to +1
           X                      X     *                0
                   X              X     *                0

id'ed      X               X            gratefully      +5
                   X       X            hesitatingly  -3 to +3
           X                      X     *                0
                   X              X     *                0

the message sequence in all cases but the (*) is like:
The gray unicorn neighs!  The gray unicorn catches the red gem.--More--
The gray unicorn (message here) accepts your gift.
where the color of the unicorn and the color of the gem are appropriate for
the situation.  (message here) comes from the table above, except where the 
column has a *, in which case the message sequence is like:
The gray unicorn neighs!--More--
The gray unicorn catches the worthless piece of red glass.--More--
The gray unicorn is not interested in your junk.
after which the unicorn will promptly drop the "gem".

