Identifying gems:
There are four methods of identifying gems:
1 - engraving : this will immediately tell you if the gem is "hard" or "soft".
                if the gem is hard (see the above list), you'll get the 
                message (for a red gem):  
                "You engrave in the floor with the red gem."
                if the gem is "soft", you'll get the message:
                "You write in the dust with the red gem."
		note that after you get the above message, you'll be prompted
		with:
		"What do you want to write in the dust here?"
		if you just type <RETURN> at this prompt, you won't waste any
		turns writing something.
2 - throwing to a unicorn : see the above section.
3 - "kickboxing" : if you put gems into a chest or large box, and then 
		   repeatedly kick the chest/box, all worthless pieces of 
		   glass will break eventually.
4 - waiting : if you wait long enough, you'll accumulate a large number of
              gems.  since the probabilities of getting worthless pieces of
	      glass are so much higher than getting true gems, you'll acquire
	      them much faster than other gems.  so, if you just wait long
	      enough, and look at all of the gems of a certain color that
	      you have, more often than not the gem of which you have the
	      most is the worthless piece of glass.  this is a bit risky, and
	      usually takes much more time than at least methods 1 and 3.

so, my usual method of identifying gems is as follows:
1 - try to engrave with it.  if you "engrave in the floor", you immediately
    know it's a true (and "hard") gem.  if you "write in the dust", it's soft, 
    and you don't know if it's a true gem, or worthless piece of glass.
    if it's a "hard" gem, #name it to a representative name.  if the gem is
    white, you know it's a diamond, if yellowish brown, it's a topaz, if
    blue, it's a sapphire.  if it's green (red), i usually name it either
    hardgreen1 (hardred1) or hardgreen2 (hardred2), since there are then
    2 possibilities as to what it is, i.e. emerald or aquamarine (ruby or
    garnet).
2 - if i can find a box or chest, use method 3, in the following way:
    for each gem:
      a - #name it something like: worthlessred, according to its color
      b - #loot the box, and put the gem(s) into it
      c - repeatedly kick the box, until about 5 to 10 kicks don't break
	  anything.
      d - #loot the box again, if gems remain (and the same number as you
	  put in), then that gem type is valuable, so i re#name the gem to
	  something like: redgemn, where n starts at 1 (for the first one
	  you find) and increments.
    then, whenever i find a gem called "worthlessred" or somesuch, i know
    it's really a worthless piece of glass.  of course, once you've found the 
    worthless type of each color, you need no longer concern yourself with 
    other gems of that color.
3 - if i can't seem to find a box, i resign myself to using method 2 or 4.
    this is pretty unusual, however.
 
note that #nameing a gem is not the same as truly identifying it (via a
scroll) for some purposes (throwing to unicorns or selling to shopkeepers).
so, early on in the game, if i have a gem that i know is a true gem, and
find a shop which will buy gems, i usually identify the gem and sell it.
(if you have a well-trained pet, this is a great way to get all of the
shopkeepers money!)


